{
  "dom_id": "step-q159273",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14243130/assets/d60800fb2b986aac396a278599651b9ee116339d4b5bbab9a7a6ae5e16f6a3b2.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-2,0)\\cup \\{1\\}$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a boundary  point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14243130/assets/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14243130/assets/b57ab5bfd2f0d685e2918c2f62601b9439475e7f29e2fbdabb912c43411a829f.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14243130/assets/f5a2dc9c52c0c24b2f611cfd3b5aa23fb068249780fd66d80039b7d548c6151a.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* an interior point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14243130/assets/3b47e92adb22c8d68cf94f947870d185ef907f9ca17c7427843e03fbcdab21cc.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'",
